 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Wang and Mueller in 2005 [S1]. It builds upon Winter's method for projective texture mapping [S1] and addresses the limitations of traditional mesh-based texture mapping, which requires filling holes and degrades with topological artifacts [S1]. The method is part of the broader field of volume texturing, which seeks to apply 2D images to 3D volumetric data for enhanced visual realism and semantic control [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is particularly relevant in computer graphics for film special effects and volume character animation [S1].
+The method was introduced by Wang and Mueller [S1]. It builds on Winter's method for projective texture mapping [Winter 2002] and uses volume rendering methods such as DSR [Jones 1999], DVR [Kajiya and Hersen 1984], and Winter's method [Winter 2002] for implementation [S1]. It differs from edge-reconstruction methods like Froumentin et al. [Froumentin et al. 2000] and Su and Willis [Su and Willis 2004] by synthesizing detail rather than reconstructing existing detail [S1]. It also differs from mesh-based methods that require filling holes [Wood et al. 2004, Guskov and Wood 2001] [S1].
 
